## How to use
COUNTING UNIT: one entry = one dependency edge, so `resolved` counts dependency edges, NOT dependency symbols or files —
the dependency symbol/file rollup in the header is the same set counted at those grains. An edge is NOT an
invocation: a symbol that uses the anchor repeatedly inside one unit contributes ONE edge here.
For the per-INVOCATION count, and for the declared signature, run `--query contract` on the module:
its `observed: N call sites` counts every call expression, so it is normally LARGER than this figure.
Neither is wrong and they are not interchangeable — pick by the question ("who depends on this" = edges,
"how many places would I have to edit" = call sites).
Resolved dependencies are the verified set (correct where grep yields false positives from
comments/strings/same-name symbols). Suggested dependencies are leads the resolver couldn't ground.
Unresolved dependencies are opaque to static analysis — grep is the right tool for those.
